    "claim_id": "IC-ARITY-ORACLE-COMPLETE-REAL-MODEL-20261101",
    "claim": "For real m >= 2, k >= 0 and beta > 0, set d=m-1-k,\nq_F=max(k,2), q_G=max(k+1,2), and H(d,q,beta)=\nmax(1-d beta,q beta). The historical costs rewrite exactly to H with q_F\nand q_G. Over beta>0, H has infimum q/(d+q), uniquely at\nbeta=1/(d+q), when d>0; infimum/minimum 1 exactly for\n0<beta<=1/q when d=0; and unattained infimum 1 when d<0. Its optimized\nvalue is below/equal/above 1/2 exactly when d is greater/equal/less than q.\nFor d>q its pointwise values are below 1/2 exactly on\n1/(2d)<beta<1/(2q) and equal 1/2 exactly at the endpoints; d=q has the\nsingle equality beta=1/(2q); d<q is pointwise above 1/2.\nThe all-return cost is pointwise no larger than the one-return cost, with\nequality exactly when k<=1 or beta<=1/m. Their optimized values are equal\nexactly when k<=1 or k>=m-1 and strictly ordered exactly when\n1<k<m-1, including equal but unattained infima above m-1.\nUnder k>=m/2 the all-return cost is pointwise above 1/2, with sharp\noptimized class bounds 4/(m+2) for 2<=m<=4 and m/(2(m-1)) for m>=4;\nthe one-return bound is 1/2+1/m. Boundary witnesses attain these class\nbounds, while an explicit growing-m schedule proves there is no uniform\npositive gap above 1/2. The aggregate proposition also contains the exact\nF1-F5 counterexamples/boundaries and the values F(5,1/5,1)=2/5 and\nF(3,1/4,0)=1/2, plus a named negation of blanket attainment.",
    "theorem_name": "CryptoResearch.IC.ArityOracleCompleteRealModel.complete_real_model",
